see what operations are available. But that might not directly indicate faults. \n\nAnother approach: check the pods and deployments to see if any are in a bad state. The exec_read_only_kubectl_cmd can be used with "kubectl get pods" and "kubectl get deployments" in the astronomy-shop namespace. That would show the current state of the workloads. If any pods are crashing or deployments are not stable, that\'s a starting point. \n\nSo, the next logical step is to check the pods and deployments. Using the exec_read_only_kubectl_cmd with "kubectl get pods" and "kubectl get deployments" in the specified namespace. This will give an overview of the current state of the application, identifying any immediately obvious issues like failed pods or deployments with errors. \n\nTherefore, the tool to use now is exec_read_only_kubectl_cmd with the command "kubectl get pods -n astronomy-shop" and then "kubectl get deployments -n astronomy-shop". But since the tool allows only one command per call, maybe start with getting the pods first. Wait, the user can make multiple tool calls. So first, get the pods, then the deployments. \n\nBut the user can only make one tool call per step. So in this step, the assistant should choose the first command, like getting the pods. Then in the next step, proceed to get deployments. \n\nSo the correct tool here is exec_read_only_kubectl_cmd with the command "kubectl get pods -n astronomy-shop". That will list all the pods in the namespace, showing their status. If any pods are in CrashLoopBackOff, Error, or similar states, that\'s a sign of a problem. \n\nAlternatively, maybe check the metrics for each service. But without knowing which service is faulty, starting with the pod status is more straightforward. So the next tool call should be to get the pods.\n</think>\n\nexec_read_only_kubectl_cmd  \nTo identify potential faults, I need to check the current state of pods and deployments in the `astronomy-shop` namespace. This tool will execute `kubectl get pods` to list all pods, revealing any that are in a non-Running state (e.g., CrashLoopBackOff, Error). This is critical for identifying immediately failing components.', additional_kwargs={'provider_specific_fields': {'refusal': None, 'reasoning': None}}, response_metadata={'token_usage': {'completion_tokens': 679, 'prompt_tokens': 1735, 'total_tokens': 2414, 'completion_tokens_details': None, 'prompt_tokens_details': None}, 'model': 'openai/Qwen/Qwen3-8B', 'finish_reason': 'stop', 'logprobs': None, 'model_name': 'openai/Qwen/Qwen3-8B'}, id='lc_run--019cfc10-e985-7f63-b50a-c73a4c8906ae-0', tool_calls=[], invalid_tool_calls=[], usage_metadata={'input_tokens': 1735, 'output_tokens': 679, 'total_tokens': 2414}), HumanMessage(content='Now generate a tool call according to your last chosen tool.', additional_kwargs={}, response_metadata={}, id='4dc087de-ae26-4f0d-9728-fdd9cfd89cbb'), AIMessage(content="<think>\nOkay, the user wants me to generate a tool call for the exec_read_only_kubectl_cmd. The previous step was to get the list of services using get_services, which gave a list of services in the astronomy-shop namespace. Now, the next logical step is to check the current state of the pods in that namespace. \n\nThe user mentioned that some or none of the microservices might have faults. To check the pods, I should use the kubectl get pods command. The exec_read_only_kubectl_cmd tool allows running read-only kubectl commands. The available commands include 'kubectl get', so I can use that to fetch the pods.\n\nI need to make sure the command is executed in the correct namespace, which is 'astronomy-shop'. The command should be 'kubectl get pods -n astronomy-shop'. This will list all the pods in that namespace, showing their current status. Any pods that are not in the 'Running' state would indicate a problem, such as CrashLoopBackOff or Error states. \n\nBy checking the pods, I can identify if there are any immediately failing components. This is a crucial step in diagnosing the application's issues. So the tool call should be to exec_read_only_kubectl_cmd with the command 'kubectl get pods -n astronomy-shop'.\n</think>\n\n", additional_kwargs={'tool_calls': [{'function': {'arguments': '{"command": "kubectl get pods -n astronomy-shop"}', 'name': 'exec_read_only_kubectl_cmd'}, 'id': 'chatcmpl-tool-a0f7d17fcff50144', 'type': 'function'}], 'provider_specific_fields': {'refusal': None, 'reasoning': None}}, response_metadata={'token_usage': {'completion_tokens': 294, 'prompt_tokens': 2716, 'total_tokens': 3010, 'completion_tokens_details': None, 'prompt_tokens_details': None}, 'model': 'openai/Qwen/Qwen3-8B', 'finish_reason': 'tool_calls', 'logprobs': None, 'model_name': 'openai/Qwen/Qwen3-8B'}, id='lc_run--019cfc11-0856-7023-a7d5-aa655df0dda1-0', tool_calls=[{'name': 'exec_read_only_kubectl_cmd', 'args': {'command': 'kubectl get pods -n astronomy-shop'}, 'id': 'chatcmpl-tool-a0f7d17fcff50144', 'type': 'tool_call'}], invalid_tool_calls=[], usage_metadata={'input_tokens': 2716, 'output_tokens': 294, 'total_tokens': 3010}), ToolMessage(content='NAME                               READY   STATUS    RESTARTS       AGE\naccounting-844d99ddff-g8hgs        1/1     Running   0              2m36s\nad-6f9964ffd8-h7vpl                1/1     Running   0              2m37s\ncart-65474cd577-nsn5v              1/1     Running   0              2m38s\ncheckout-648b497649-m54vg          1/1     Running   0              2m36s\ncurrency-69f5b55bbc-tvvkh          1/1     Running   0              2m37s\nemail-56985bb4f8-nqxvq             1/1     Running   0              2m37s\nflagd-79c4c6cb44-b8gnv             2/2     Running   0              2m35s\nfraud-detection-88b76487c-hmznb    1/1     Running   0              2m37s\nfrontend-55df67b4b9-k9rmj          1/1     Running   1 (11s ago)    34s\nfrontend-proxy-84788b44b-hjp8s     1/1     Running   0              2m35s\ngrafana-bdbfb995f-ch474            4/4     Running   0              2m35s\nima