2026-03-17 13:53:22 - DEBUG - all.stratus.diagnosis - [Loop 7] Tool call - base_agent.py:llm_tool_call_step:102 [Full Prompt = [SystemMessage(content="Monitor and diagnose an application consisting of **MANY** microservices. Some or none of the microservices have faults. Get all the pods and deployments to figure out what kind of services are running in the cluster.  Carefully identify the whether the faults are present and if they are, and identify what is the root cause of the fault.\nStop diagnosis once you've found the root cause of the faults.\nGo as deep as you can into what is causing the issue.\nYour instructions to the tools must be clear and concise. Your queries to tools need to be single turn.\nRemember to check these, and remember this information: ## Workloads (Applications) - **Pod**: The smallest deployable unit in Kubernetes, representing a single instance of a running application. Can contain one or more tightly coupled containers. - **ReplicaSet**: Ensures that a specified number of pod replicas are running at all times. Often managed indirectly through Deployments. - **Deployment**: Manages the deployment and lifecycle of applications. Provides declarative updates for Pods and ReplicaSets. - **StatefulSet**: Manages stateful applications with unique pod identities and stable storage. Used for workloads like databases. - **DaemonSet**: Ensures that a copy of a specific pod runs on every node in the cluster. Useful for node monitoring agents, log collectors, etc. - **Job**: Manages batch processing tasks that are expected to complete successfully. Ensures pods run to completion. - **CronJob**: Schedules jobs to run at specified times or intervals (similar to cron in Linux).\n## Networking - **Service**: Provides a stable network endpoint for accessing a group of pods. Types: ClusterIP, NodePort, LoadBalancer, and ExternalName. - **Ingress**: Manages external HTTP(S) access to services in the cluster. Supports routing and load balancing for HTTP(S) traffic. - **NetworkPolicy**: Defines rules for network communication between pods and other entities. Used for security and traffic control.\n## Storage - **PersistentVolume (PV)**: Represents a piece of storage in the cluster, provisioned by an administrator or dynamically. - **PersistentVolumeClaim (PVC)**: Represents a request for storage by a user. Binds to a PersistentVolume. - **StorageClass**: Defines different storage tiers or backends for dynamic provisioning of PersistentVolumes. - **ConfigMap**: Stores configuration data as key-value pairs for applications. - **Secret**: Stores sensitive data like passwords, tokens, or keys in an encrypted format.\n## Configuration and Metadata - **Namespace**: Logical partitioning of resources within the cluster for isolation and organization. - **ConfigMap**: Provides non-sensitive configuration data in key-value format. - **Secret**: Stores sensitive configuration data securely. - **ResourceQuota**: Restricts resource usage (e.g., CPU, memory) within a namespace. - **LimitRange**: Enforces minimum and maximum resource limits for containers in a namespace.\n## Cluster Management - **Node**: Represents a worker machine in the cluster (virtual or physical). Runs pods and is managed by the control plane. - **ClusterRole and Role**: Define permissions for resources at the cluster or namespace level. - **ClusterRoleBinding and RoleBinding**: Bind roles to users or groups for authorization. - **ServiceAccount**: Associates processes in pods with permissions for accessing the Kubernetes API.\nAfter you finished, submit the detected faulty components to the orchestrator for evaluation. The submission should be a natural language description of the root cause of the failure.\n", additional_kwargs={}, response_metadata={}, id='bfc59c9a-ee3d-4409-9701-593e838b43c1'), HumanMessage(content="You will be working this application:\nOpenTelemetry Demo Astronomy Shop\nHere are some descriptions about the application:\nAn online shopping platform built with a microservices architecture, showcasing OpenTelemetry instrumentation for distributed tracing across services.\nIt belongs to this namespace:\nastronomy-shop\nIn each round, there is a thinking stage. In the thinking stage, you are given a list of tools. Think about what you want to call. Return your tool choice and the reasoning behind When choosing the tool, refer to the tool by its name. Then, there is a tool-call stage, where you make a tool_call consistent with your explanation. You can run up to 20 rounds to finish the tasks. If you call submit_tool in tool-call stage, the process will end immediately. If you exceed this limitation, the system will force you to make a submission. You will begin by analyzing the service's state and telemetry with the tools.\n", additional_kwargs={}, response_metadata={}, id='b4467633-879c-4c6b-87be-ce17685d0d5c'), HumanMessage(content='You are now in the thinking stage. Here are all the tools you can use:\ntool name: get_traces\n\ntool description: Get Jaeger traces for a given service in the last n minutes.\n\n    Args:\n        service (str): The name of the service for which to retrieve trace data.\n        last_n_minutes (int): The time range (in minutes) to look back from the current time.\n\n\ntool name: get_services\n\ntool description: Retrieve the list of service names from the Grafana instance.\n\n    Args:\n\n    Returns:\n        List[str]: A list of service names available in Grafana.\n\n\ntool name: get_operations\n\ntool description: Query available operations for a specific service from the Grafana instance.\n\n    Args:\n        service (str): The name of the service whose operations should be retrieved.\n\n    Returns:\n        List[str]: A list of operation names associated with the specified service.\n\n\ntool name: get_dependency_graph\n\ntool description: Get service dependency graph from Jaeger\'s native dependencies API.\n    Args:\n        last_n_minutes (int): The time range (in minutes) to look back from the current time.\n    Returns:\n        str: JSON object representing the dependency graph.\n\n\ntool name: get_metrics\n\ntool description: Query real-time metrics data from the Prometheus instance.\n\n    Args:\n        query (str): A Prometheus Query Language (PromQL) expression used to fetch metric values.\n\n    Returns:\n        dict: The raw Prometheus response containing metric results, including timestamps, values, and labels.\n\n\ntool name: exec_read_only_kubectl_cmd\n\ntool description: This is a tool used to execute read-only kubectl commands.\n\n    Args:\n        command (str): The read-only kubectl command you want to execute in a CLI to manage a k8s cluster. It should start with "kubectl".\n        Available Read-only Commands:\n        "kubectl api-resources",\n        "kubectl api-version",\n        "kubectl auth can-i",\n        "kubectl cluster-info",\n        "kubectl describe",\n        "kubectl diff",\n        "kubectl events",\n        "kubectl explain",\n        "kubectl get",\n        "kubectl logs",\n        "kubectl options",\n        "kubectl top",\n        "kubectl version",\n        "kubectl config view",\n        "kubectl config current-context",\n        "kubectl config get",\n        INTERACTIVE COMMANDS ARE NOT SUPPORTED, e.g. "kubectl edit"\n        INTERACTIVE FLAGS ARE NOT SUPPORTED, e.g., "kubectl logs -f"\n\n\ntool name: submit_tool\n\ntool description: The tool to submit benchmark results\n\n    Args:\n        ans (str): the answer you would like to submit to the benchmark. Use natural language to describe the root cause of the failure.\n\n\nChoose a tool from the list and output the tool name. Justify your tool choice. In the next step, you will generate a tool call for this tool', additional_kwargs={}, response_metadata={}, id='ed8b99c1-71ba-420a-85a6-a29eb0adad9b'), AIMessage(content="<think>\nOkay, let's start by understanding the problem. The user wants me to monitor and diagnose an application called OpenTelemetry Demo Astronomy Shop, which is a microservices-based platform. The goal is to identify any faults and determine their root causes. \n\nFirst, I need to figure out what services are running in the cluster. The application is in the namespace 'astronomy-shop'. The tools available include 'get_services' which retrieves service names from Grafana. That seems like a good starting point because knowing the services will help me check each one for issues.\n\nSo, I'll use 'get_services' to list all the services in the Grafana instance. Once I have the list, I can then check each service's metrics, traces, and dependencies to see if there are any anomalies. For example, if a service is down or has high error rates, that could indicate a problem. \n\nAfter getting the services, maybe I'll use 'get_metrics' to look at real-time metrics like error rates or latency. If there are spikes in errors or high latency, that could point to faulty services. Then, using 'get_traces' for specific services to see if there are any failed transactions or long-running requests. \n\nAlso, the 'get_dependency_graph' could show if there are any broken dependencies between services. If a service isn't communicating properly with others, that might be a root cause. \n\nBut first, I need the list of services. So the first step is to call 'get_services' to get all the service names in the 'astronomy-shop' namespace. That way, I can systematically check each one.\n</think>\n\nget_services  \nTo begin diagnosing the application, I need to identify all the services running in the 'astronomy-shop' namespace. The `get_services` tool will retrieve the list of service names from Grafana, providing a foundation for further analysis of their health, metrics, and dependencies. This is the first step in understanding the application's architecture and identifying potential fault points.", additional_kwargs={'provider_specific_fields': {'refusal': None, 'reasoning': None}}, response_metadata={'token_usage': {'completion_tokens': 411, 'prompt_tokens': 1492, 'total_tokens': 1903, 'completion_tokens_details': None, 'prompt_tokens_details': None}, 'model': 'openai/Qwen/Qwen3-8B', 'finish_reason': 'stop', 'logprobs': None, 'model_name': 'openai/Qwen/Qwen3-8B'}, id='lc_run--019cfc10-ba50-7371-b76e-99b227a508fb-0', tool_calls=[], invalid_tool_calls=[], usage_metadata={'input_tokens': 1492, 'output_tokens': 411, 'total_tokens': 1903}), HumanMessage(content='Now generate a tool call according to your last chosen tool.', additional_kwargs={}, response_metadata={}, id='97c9ec58-1a6f-4ce4-b741-94de951b7375'), AIMessage(content="<think>\nOkay, let's see. The user wants me to monitor and diagnose an application with many microservices. The first step is to figure out what services are running in the cluster, specifically in the 'astronomy-shop' namespace. The user mentioned using the get_services tool, which retrieves service names from Grafana. \n\nWait, but the tool description says it gets the list from Grafana. However, the application in question is the OpenTelemetry Demo Astronomy Shop, which is in the astronomy-shop namespace. Should I specify the namespace when using get_services? The tool's parameters don't mention a namespace argument. Hmm, maybe the Grafana instance is already scoped to that namespace, or perhaps the tool doesn't require it. The user's initial instructions say to check the namespace, so maybe I need to confirm that.\n\nBut the get_services tool's parameters are empty, so it doesn't take any arguments. That means it might return all services in the cluster, not just the astronomy-shop namespace. That could be a problem. Wait, maybe the Grafana instance the tool is querying is already filtered to the correct namespace. Alternatively, maybe I need to use kubectl to list the services in the namespace first. But the user hasn't mentioned using kubectl yet. \n\nWait, the user's initial setup says the application belongs to the astronomy-shop namespace. So perhaps the get_services tool, when called, will return services specific to that namespace. But since the tool's parameters don't include a namespace, maybe it's designed to work within that context. Alternatively, maybe I need to use the exec_read_only_kubectl_cmd to get the services in the namespace. For example, running 'kubectl get services -n astronomy-shop'. \n\nBut the user's first step was to use get_services. Maybe the Grafana instance is already set up to show services in that namespace. However, if the tool doesn't take a namespace parameter, it might not be accurate. But since the user instructed to start with get_services, I should proceed with that. \n\nSo the tool call would be to get_services with no arguments. The response would give a list of services. Then, based on that list, I can proceed to check each service's health, metrics, dependencies, etc. \n\nAlternatively, if the get_services tool isn't scoped to the namespace, the list might include services from other namespaces, which would complicate things. But given the user's setup, perhaps the Grafana instance is already configured for the astronomy-shop namespace. \n\nIn any case, the first step is to get the list of services. So the correct tool call is get_services with no arguments. Then, once I have the services, I can move on to checking their metrics, traces, and dependencies.\n</think>\n\n", additional_kwargs={'tool_calls': [{'function': {'arguments': '{}', 'name': 'get_services'}, 'id': 'chatcmpl-tool-9c4349bf5ff8b0d1', 'type': 'function'}], 'provider_specific_fields': {'refusal': None, 'reasoning': None}}, response_metadata={'token_usage': {'completion_tokens': 579, 'prompt_tokens': 2473, 'total_tokens': 3052, 'completion_tokens_details': None, 'prompt_tokens_details': None}, 'model': 'openai/Qwen/Qwen3-8B', 'finish_reason': 'tool_calls', 'logprobs': None, 'model_name': 'openai/Qwen/Qwen3-8B'}, id='lc_run--019cfc10-cdf7-77b0-9729-579cc5921159-0', tool_calls=[{'name': 'get_services', 'args': {}, 'id': 'chatcmpl-tool-9c4349bf5ff8b0d1', 'type': 'tool_call'}], invalid_tool_calls=[], usage_metadata={'input_tokens': 2473, 'output_tokens': 579, 'total_tokens': 3052}), ToolMessage(content='meta=None content=[TextContent(type=\'text\', text="[\'frontend-proxy\', \'checkout\', \'product-reviews\', \'fraud-detection\', \'payment\', \'jaeger-all-in-one\', \'load-generator\', \'ad\', \'frontend\', \'email\', \'image-provider\', \'currency\', \'product-catalog\', \'recommendation\', \'cart\', \'flagd\', \'quote\', \'shipping\', \'accounting\']", annotations=None)] isError=False', id='c4593a17-a17f-4274-8bc7-37c22fb384b6', tool_call_id='chatcmpl-tool-9c4349bf5ff8b0d1'), HumanMessage(content='You are now in the thinking stage. Choose a tool from the available tools and justify your choice.', additional_kwargs={}, response_metadata={}, id='0efe76d0-3589-4e99-bee4-18fa044b882c'), AIMessage(content='<think>\nOkay, so the user is trying to diagnose issues in the OpenTelemetry Demo Astronomy Shop, which is a microservices application. They\'ve already used the get_services tool to list all the services in the namespace. The next step is to figure out which of these services might be faulty.\n\nFirst, I need to recall the tools available. There\'s get_traces, get_dependency_graph, get_metrics, and others. Since the user mentioned that some or none of the microservices have faults, I should probably start by checking the health of each service. But how?\n\nThe get_metrics tool can query Prometheus for real-time metrics. Maybe I can check for errors or latency issues. Alternatively, the get_traces tool could help identify issues in the distributed tracing. But without knowing which service is problematic, it\'s hard to narrow down. \n\nWait, the get_dependency_graph might show dependencies between services. If there\'s a service that\'s not responding, the dependency graph could highlight that. But the user might need to check each service\'s metrics first. \n\nAlternatively, using get_operations on each service to see what operations are available. But that might not directly indicate faults. \n\nAnother approach: check the pods and deployments to see if any are in a bad state. The exec_read_only_kubectl_cmd can be used with "kubectl get pods" and "kubectl get deployments" in the astronomy-shop namespace. That would show the current state of the workloads. If any pods are crashing or deployments are not stable, that\'s a starting point. \n\nSo, the next logical step is to check the pods and deployments. Using the exec_read_only_kubectl_cmd with "kubectl get pods" and "kubectl get deployments" in the specified namespace. This will give an overview of the current state of the application, identifying any immediately obvious issues like failed pods or deployments with errors. \n\nTherefore, the tool to use now is exec_read_only_kubectl_cmd with the command "kubectl get pods -n astronomy-shop" and then "kubectl get deployments -n astronomy-shop". But since the tool allows only one command per call, maybe start with getting the pods first. Wait, the user can make multiple tool calls. So first, get the pods, then the deployments. \n\nBut the user can only make one tool call per step. So in this step, the assistant should choose the first command, like getting the pods. Then in the next step, proceed to get deployments. \n\nSo the correct tool here is exec_read_only_kubectl_cmd with the command "kubectl get pods -n astronomy-shop". That will list all the pods in the namespace, showing their status. If any pods are in CrashLoopBackOff, Error, or similar states, that\'s a sign of a problem. \n\nAlternatively, maybe check the metrics for each service. But without knowing which service is faulty, starting with the pod status is more straightforward. So the next tool call should be to get the pods.\n</think>\n\nexec_read_only_kubectl_cmd  \nTo identify potential faults, I need to check the current state of pods and deployments in the `astronomy-shop` namespace. This tool will execute `kubectl get pods` to list all pods, revealing any that are in a non-Running state (e.g., CrashLoopBackOff, Error). This is critical for identifying immediately failing components.', additional_kwargs={'provider_specific_fields': {'refusal': None, 'reasoning': None}}, response_metadata={'token_usage': {'completion_tokens': 679, 'prompt_tokens': 1735, 'total_tokens': 2414, 'completion_tokens_details': None, 'prompt_tokens_details': None}, 'model': 'openai/Qwen/Qwen3-8B', 'finish_reason': 'stop', 'logprobs': None, 'model_name': 'openai/Qwen/Qwen3-8B'}, id='lc_run--019cfc10-e985-7f63-b50a-c73a4c8906ae-0', tool_calls=[], invalid_tool_calls=[], usage_metadata={'input_tokens': 1735, 'output_tokens': 679, 'total_tokens': 2414}), HumanMessage(content='Now generate a tool call according to your last chosen tool.', additional_kwargs={}, response_metadata={}, id='4dc087de-ae26-4f0d-9728-fdd9cfd89cbb'), AIMessage(content="<think>\nOkay, the user wants me to generate a tool call for the exec_read_only_kubectl_cmd. The previous step was to get the list of services using get_services, which gave a list of services in the astronomy-shop namespace. Now, the next logical step is to check the current state of the pods in that namespace. \n\nThe user mentioned that some or none of the microservices might have faults. To check the pods, I should use the kubectl get pods command. The exec_read_only_kubectl_cmd tool allows running read-only kubectl commands. The available commands include 'kubectl get', so I can use that to fetch the pods.\n\nI need to make sure the command is executed in the correct namespace, which is 'astronomy-shop'. The command should be 'kubectl get pods -n astronomy-shop'. This will list all the pods in that namespace, showing their current status. Any pods that are not in the 'Running' state would indicate a problem, such as CrashLoopBackOff or Error states. \n\nBy checking the pods, I can identify if there are any immediately failing components. This is a crucial step in diagnosing the application's issues. So the tool call should be to exec_read_only_kubectl_cmd with the command 'kubectl get pods -n astronomy-shop'.\n</think>\n\n", additional_kwargs={'tool_calls': [{'function': {'arguments': '{"command": "kubectl get pods -n astronomy-shop"}', 'name': 'exec_read_only_kubectl_cmd'}, 'id': 'chatcmpl-tool-a0f7d17fcff50144', 'type': 'function'}], 'provider_specific_fields': {'refusal': None, 'reasoning': None}}, response_metadata={'token_usage': {'completion_tokens': 294, 'prompt_tokens': 2716, 'total_tokens': 3010, 'completion_tokens_details': None, 'prompt_tokens_details': None}, 'model': 'openai/Qwen/Qwen3-8B', 'finish_reason': 'tool_calls', 'logprobs': None, 'model_name': 'openai/Qwen/Qwen3-8B'}, id='lc_run--019cfc11-0856-7023-a7d5-aa655df0dda1-0', tool_calls=[{'name': 'exec_read_only_kubectl_cmd', 'args': {'command': 'kubectl get pods -n astronomy-shop'}, 'id': 'chatcmpl-tool-a0f7d17fcff50144', 'type': 'tool_call'}], invalid_tool_calls=[], usage_metadata={'input_tokens': 2716, 'output_tokens': 294, 'total_tokens': 3010}), ToolMessage(content='NAME                               READY   STATUS    RESTARTS       AGE\naccounting-844d99ddff-g8hgs        1/1     Running   0              2m36s\nad-6f9964ffd8-h7vpl                1/1     Running   0              2m37s\ncart-65474cd577-nsn5v              1/1     Running   0              2m38s\ncheckout-648b497649-m54vg          1/1     Running   0              2m36s\ncurrency-69f5b55bbc-tvvkh          1/1     Running   0              2m37s\nemail-56985bb4f8-nqxvq             1/1     Running   0              2m37s\nflagd-79c4c6cb44-b8gnv             2/2     Running   0              2m35s\nfraud-detection-88b76487c-hmznb    1/1     Running   0              2m37s\nfrontend-55df67b4b9-k9rmj          1/1     Running   1 (11s ago)    34s\nfrontend-proxy-84788b44b-hjp8s     1/1     Running   0              2m35s\ngrafana-bdbfb995