me might have a random suffix, maybe using kubectl logs for the frontend deployment\'s pods would be better. Alternatively, they could check the specific pod that\'s crashing.\n\nWait, the user already ran kubectl get pods and saw the frontend-55df67b4b9-k9rmj pod with 5 restarts. So, using that exact pod name in the logs command would be more precise. But if the pod name isn\'t known, maybe using the deployment name with --selector or --pod-name. However, the tool requires the exact command. So the correct command would be kubectl logs frontend-55df67b4b9-k9rmj -n astronomy-shop to get the logs. That should show any errors related to the health endpoint or application startup issues.\n</think>\n\ntool name: exec_kubectl_cmd_safely\n\nReasoning: The frontend pod is in CrashLoopBackOff due to a liveness probe failure on /healthz. To diagnose the root cause, we need to inspect the pod\'s logs to identify why the health endpoint isn\'t responding. This will reveal if the application is failing to start, misconfigured, or if there\'s an issue with the endpoint itself.\n\ntool_call: {"command": "kubectl logs frontend-55df67b4b9-k9rmj -n astronomy-shop"}', additional_kwargs={'provider_specific_fields': {'refusal': None, 'reasoning': None}}, response_metadata={'token_usage': {'completion_tokens': 479, 'prompt_tokens': 3289, 'total_tokens': 3768, 'completion_tokens_details': None, 'prompt_tokens_details': None}, 'model': 'openai/Qwen/Qwen3-8B', 'finish_reason': 'stop', 'logprobs': None, 'model_name': 'openai/Qwen/Qwen3-8B'}, id='lc_run--019cfc12-e85e-7e70-b71a-85396faa3afd-0', tool_calls=[], invalid_tool_calls=[], usage_metadata={'input_tokens': 3289, 'output_tokens': 479, 'total_tokens': 3768}), HumanMessage(content='Now generate a tool call according to your last chosen tool.', additional_kwargs={}, response_metadata={}, id='cf8daab6-a40b-4afa-8d28-ff4a50e0e152'), AIMessage(content='<think>\nOkay, let me try to figure out what\'s going on here. The user is dealing with the frontend service in the Astronomy Shop application that\'s crashing repeatedly. From the previous steps, they checked the pods and saw that the frontend pod is in CrashLoopBackOff. The liveness probe is failing with a 404 error on /healthz.\n\nSo, the next step is to check the logs of the frontend pod to see why the health endpoint isn\'t responding. The user tried using kubectl to get the logs, but maybe the pod name is different. Wait, in the previous response, the pod name was frontend-55df67b4b9-k9rmj. The user should use that exact name to get the logs. \n\nBut the user might not know the exact pod name, so they need to retrieve it first. However, in the previous tool call, they used "kubectl get pods" and saw the frontend pod\'s name. So the next step is to execute "kubectl logs" with the correct pod name and namespace. \n\nThe command should be "kubectl logs frontend-55df67b4b9-k9rmj -n astronomy-shop". This will show the logs from the crashing pod, which should indicate why the /healthz endpoint isn\'t working. If the logs show that the application isn\'t starting properly or the endpoint isn\'t configured, that\'s the root cause. \n\nAlternatively, maybe the application is crashing before it can start the health endpoint. The logs should clarify that. Once the logs are retrieved, the user can analyze them to determine the exact issue and take corrective action, like fixing the configuration or ensuring the endpoint is correctly set up.\n</think>\n\n', additional_kwargs={'tool_calls': [{'function': {'arguments': '{"command": "kubectl logs frontend-55df67b4b9-k9rmj -n astronomy-shop"}', 'name': 'exec_kubectl_cmd_safely'}, 'id': 'chatcmpl-tool-bc414bc675684fc7', 'type': 'function'}], 'provider_specific_fields': {'refusal': None, 'reasoning': None}}, response_metadata={'token_usage': {'completion_tokens': 396, 'prompt_tokens': 4427, 'total_tokens': 4823, 'completion_tokens_details': None, 'prompt_tokens_details': None}, 'model': 'openai/Qwen/Qwen3-8B', 'finish_reason': 'tool_calls', 'logprobs': None, 'model_name': 'openai/Qwen/Qwen3-8B'}, id='lc_run--019cfc12-fef2-7211-be3f-197122a49ef3-0', tool_calls=[{'name': 'exec_kubectl_cmd_safely', 'args': {'command': 'kubectl logs frontend-55df67b4b9-k9rmj -n astronomy-shop'}, 'id': 'chatcmpl-tool-bc414bc675684fc7', 'type': 'tool_call'}], invalid_tool_calls=[], usage_metadata={'input_tokens': 4427, 'output_tokens': 396, 'total_tokens': 4823}), ToolMessage(content='▲ Next.js 16.1.1\n- Local:         http://frontend-55df67b4b9-k9rmj:8080\n- Network:       http://frontend-55df67b4b9-k9rmj:8080\n\n✓ Starting...\n✓ Ready in 169ms\n', id='a0675fd8-0d86-42a6-bc10-cc96a2966c2d', tool_call_id='chatcmpl-tool-bc414bc675684fc7'), HumanMessage(content='You are now in the thinking stage. Choose a tool from the available tools and justify your choice.', additional_kwargs={}, response_metadata={}, id='90a039d6-fb0b-4a98-874c-35acf19dda24'), AIMessage(content='<think>\nOkay, let\'s see. The user is trying to figure out why the frontend service in the OpenTelemetry Demo Astronomy Shop is restarting. The previous steps showed that the frontend pod is in CrashLoopBackOff because the liveness probe is failing with a 404 on /healthz. The user checked the pod logs, and the output shows the app started but maybe didn\'t set up the health endpoint properly.\n\nSo, the next step is to check the liveness probe configuration. The logs don\'t show any errors, but the probe is still failing. Maybe the probe\'s path is incorrect. The user needs to verify the probe settings in the deployment YAML. If the healthz endpoint isn\'t exposed, the probe can\'t reach it, causing the pod to restart.\n\nThe available tools include exec_kubectl_cmd_safely. The user can use kubectl to describe the deployment or check the pod\'s configuration. Alternatively, they might need to check the application\'s code to see if /healthz is implemented. But since the user can\'t access the code directly, checking the deployment\'s liveness probe settings is the next best step. So, the tool call should be to get the deployment\'s YAML to inspect the probe configuration.\n</think>\n\ntool name: exec_kubectl_cmd_safely\n\nReasoning: The logs show the frontend application started but the liveness probe still fails. This suggests the `/healthz` endpoint may not be properly configured or exposed. We need to inspect the deployment configuration to verify the liveness probe settings and ensure the endpoint is correctly defined in the application.\n\ntool_call: {"command": "kubectl get deployment frontend -n astronomy-shop -o yaml"}', additional_kwargs={'provider_specific_fields': {'refusal': None, 'reasoning': None}}, response_metadata={'token_usage': {'completion_tokens': 344, 'prompt_tokens': 3583, 'total_tokens': 3927, 'completion_tokens_details': None, 'prompt_tokens_details': None}, 'model': 'openai/Qwen/Qwen3-8B', 'finish_reason': 'stop', 'logprobs': None, 'model_name': 'openai/Qwen/Qwen3-8B'}, id='lc_run--019cfc13-151c-7981-ba66-8bdc85193230-0', tool_calls=[], invalid_tool_calls=[], usage_metadata={'input_tokens': 3583, 'output_tokens': 344, 'total_tokens': 3927}), HumanMessage(content='Now generate a tool call according to your last chosen tool.', additional_kwargs={}, response_metadata={}, id='2d3ff670-063c-4a14-b01a-3ea28b5364b0'), AIMessage(content="<think>\nOkay, let me try to figure out what's going on here. The user is dealing with the frontend service in the Astronomy Shop application that's restarting due to a liveness probe failure. The logs show the app starts but the /healthz endpoint isn't responding. The previous step was checking the deployment configuration for the frontend.\n\nThe user called `kubectl get deployment frontend -n astronomy-shop -o yaml` to check the liveness probe settings. Now, they need to analyze the output of that command. The response from the tool would show the deployment's YAML configuration, including the liveness probe details. \n\nLooking at the deployment YAML, the liveness probe is set to check http://localhost:8080/healthz. But the application might not be serving this endpoint. Maybe the endpoint is misconfigured, or the application isn't set up to handle /healthz. Alternatively, the probe might be using the wrong port or path. \n\nThe next step is to verify if the /healthz endpoint is actually available. The user could check the pod's logs again to see if there are any errors related to the health endpoint. Alternatively, they could use `kubectl exec` to curl the endpoint from within the pod. \n\nAnother possibility is that the application's configuration is missing the /healthz route. The user might need to check the application's code or configuration files to ensure the endpoint is properly set up. If the endpoint is correct, maybe the liveness probe's initial delay or timeout settings are too aggressive, causing the probe to fail before the app is ready.\n\nSince the user has access to the deployment YAML, they should look for the livenessProbe section. If the path is incorrect, they can update it. If the port is wrong, adjust that. Also, checking the readinessProbe might help if the issue is related to the app not being ready before the probe starts.\n\nIn the next tool call, the user should probably check the pod's logs again for any errors related to the health endpoint or the liveness probe. Alternatively, they could test the endpoint directly using `kubectl exec` to see if it's responding. This would help confirm if the issue is with the probe configuration or the application itself.\n</think>\n\n", additional_kwargs={'tool_calls': [{'function': {'arguments': '{"command": "kubectl get deployment frontend -n astronomy-shop -o yaml"}', 'name': 'exec_kubectl_cmd_safely'}, 'id': 'chatcmpl-tool-888bdcc3accd803f', 'type': 'function'}], 'provider_specific_fields': {'refusal': None, 'reasoning': None}}, response_metadata={'token_usage': {'completion_tokens': 490, 'prompt_tokens': 4697, 'total_tokens': 5187, 'co