e issue persists. The latest describe pod output shows that the container is crashing because the health endpoint isn\'t found.\n\nHmm, the liveness probe is set to check http://localhost:8080/healthz. But the application might not be serving that endpoint. Maybe the app is configured to use a different path or the endpoint isn\'t implemented. Alternatively, the application might be crashing before it starts the server, so the endpoint isn\'t available.\n\nWait, the user tried to curl the endpoint inside the pod and got an error. The previous attempts to exec into the pod failed due to WebSocket issues, but the describe pod shows the container is crashing. So the application might not be starting correctly. Maybe the app\'s entry point script isn\'t running, or there\'s a misconfiguration in the Docker image.\n\nLooking at the deployment YAML, the container\'s image is ghcr.io/open-telemetry/demo:2.2.0-frontend. Maybe the image is missing the /healthz endpoint. Or perhaps the application is configured to use a different port. The logs from the pod weren\'t shown earlier, but the describe pod indicates the container exited with code 0, which might mean it completed but didn\'t start the server properly.\n\nWait, the Last State shows the container was Terminated with Exit Code 0, started at 13:53:35, finished at 13:53:51. That\'s a very short runtime. So the container might be starting but immediately exiting because the health endpoint isn\'t available. The liveness probe is set to check /healthz, which isn\'t served, causing the probe to fail and the container to restart.\n\nSo the root cause is that the frontend application isn\'t serving the /healthz endpoint. This could be due to the application not having that endpoint, or the endpoint being misconfigured. To fix this, we need to either adjust the liveness probe to the correct endpoint or modify the application to serve /healthz. Alternatively, check if the application is configured to run on a different port or if there\'s a misconfiguration in the Docker image.\n\nSince the user can\'t modify the image, the best option is to adjust the liveness probe to the correct path. But how? The deployment YAML shows the liveness probe is set to /healthz. If the application doesn\'t have that endpoint, changing it to a different path like / or another endpoint that\'s available might help. Alternatively, check if the application is using a different port, but the probe is set to port 8080, which matches the container\'s port.\n\nWait, the user\'s previous attempt to curl http://localhost:8080/healthz from inside the pod might have failed because the app isn\'t serving that path. So the solution is to either fix the application to serve /healthz or change the probe\'s path to a valid endpoint. Since the user can\'t modify the application, maybe the probe\'s path is incorrect. Let me check the deployment again.\n\nIn the deployment YAML, the livenessProbe\'s httpGet has path /healthz and port 8080. If the application doesn\'t have that endpoint, changing the path to something else, like /, might work. But how to confirm? The user could check the application\'s documentation or source code. Since that\'s not possible, the next step is to adjust the probe\'s path to a known working endpoint.\n\nAlternatively, maybe the application is using a different port. But the container\'s port is set to 8080, and the probe is using that. So the port is correct. Therefore, the issue is the missing /healthz endpoint. The solution would be to update the livenessProbe\'s path to a valid endpoint, like /, or modify the application to include /healthz.\n\nBut since the user can\'t modify the application, the best option is to adjust the probe\'s path. So the next step is to edit the deployment to change the livenessProbe\'s path to a valid endpoint. However, the user might not have access to edit the deployment. Alternatively, check if the application is configured to use a different path for health checks.\n\nWait, the user has the ability to use the exec_kubectl_cmd_safely tool. Maybe they can check the application\'s configuration files inside the pod. For example, check if there\'s a health endpoint configured in the app\'s code or environment variables. But the previous attempt to exec into the pod failed due to WebSocket errors, but the describe pod shows the container is crashing. So maybe the container isn\'t running, making it impossible to check.\n\nAlternatively, the user could try to check the application\'s logs again. The previous logs showed the app starting but maybe not serving the endpoint. The user could use kubectl logs to get more detailed logs from the pod. Wait, the user already tried that earlier and saw the app starting but no further info. Maybe the logs are not detailed enough.\n\nAnother approach: check the application\'s documentation. Since the app is part of the OpenTelemetry Demo, maybe the health endpoint is different. For example, maybe it\'s /health instead of /healthz. If that\'s the case, changing the probe\'s path to /health would resolve the issue.\n\nSo the root cause is the liveness probe is checking the wrong endpoint. The fix is to update the livenessProbe\'s path in the deployment to the correct endpoint. The user needs to edit the deployment YAML to change the path from /healthz to the correct one, like /health or another valid endpoint.\n\nTherefore, the next step is to modify the deployment\'s livenessProbe configuration to use the correct path. The user can do this by updating the deployment resource.\n</think>\n\ntool name: exec_kubectl_cmd_safely\n\nReasoning: The liveness probe is failing because the `/healthz` endpoint is not available. The root cause is likely the absence of this endpoint in the application. To resolve this, we need to update the liveness probe configuration in the deployment to use a valid endpoint (e.g., `/` or another known path). This will prevent the probe from failing and allow the container to remain running.\n\ntool_call: {"command": "kubectl edit deployment frontend -n astronomy-shop"}', additional_kwargs={'provider_specific_fields': {'refusal': None, 'reasoning': None}}, response_metadata={'token_usage': Usage(completion_tokens=1338, prompt_tokens=8450, total_tokens=9788, completion_tokens_details=None, prompt_tokens_details=None), 'model': 'openai/Qwen/Qwen3-8B', 'finish_reason': 'stop', 'logprobs': None, 'model_name': 'openai/Qwen/Qwen3-8B'}, id='lc_run--019cfc13-ecd4-7c01-aebe-005a887a16c1-0', tool_calls=[], invalid_tool_calls=[], usage_metadata={'input_tokens': 8450, 'output_tokens': 1338, 'total_tokens': 9788}), HumanMessage(content='Now generate a tool call according to your last chosen tool.', additional_kwargs={}, response_metadata={}, id='9d0049ad-5820-4a98-af0a-8a5650bad8a3')]]
